    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
    <!--  FAQ                                                          -->
    <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <section class="section" id="faq">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <!-- ── Before You Start (trust section) ──────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Before You Start</h3>

      <details class="faq-item" open>
        <summary>Will I lose control of my codebase?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. Everything runs in your terminal, in your Git repo. You see every change, approve every commit, stop any agent at any time. Run <code>/profile</code> to set your autonomy level &mdash; from fully supervised to fully autonomous.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Is my code private?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes. VibeCrew runs entirely locally. Your code goes to the Claude API (same as vanilla Claude Code) and nowhere else. No external servers, no telemetry, no data collection. Open source &mdash; verify for yourself.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What if VibeCrew makes a mistake?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Every change goes through Git. The quality gate catches type errors, lint issues, and build failures after every task. Review diffs before committing, revert commits with <code>git revert</code>, or use <code>/undo</code> to roll back an entire feature.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can I stop using VibeCrew and keep my code?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Yes. Your project is standard code in your chosen framework. Remove the plugin symlink, delete the <code>.vibecrew/</code> folder, and you have a normal codebase. No lock-in, no proprietary formats, no generated code that depends on VibeCrew to run.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ── Getting Started ───────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Getting Started</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is VibeCrew?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>VibeCrew is a Claude Code plugin that transforms Claude Code into an autonomous development team. It orchestrates multiple AI agents, enforces architecture-first development, and manages the full software development lifecycle &mdash; from project vision to deployment.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Do I need coding experience to use VibeCrew?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. VibeCrew is designed for users at all skill levels. Run <code>/profile</code> to set your experience level, and VibeCrew adapts its explanations, autonomy, and output style accordingly. Non-technical founders can build production-grade apps using the Founder preset.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What terminals does VibeCrew support?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>VibeCrew works with any terminal. Warp Terminal is recommended because it provides tab deep-linking (click a notification to jump to the exact session), structured output blocks, and Launch Configurations. But iTerm2, VS Code Terminal, Terminal.app, and others all work fine.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I install VibeCrew on multiple projects?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Clone VibeCrew once to a central location (e.g., <code>~/claude-plugins/vibe-crew/</code>), then symlink into each project: <code>ln -s ~/claude-plugins/vibe-crew/claude-plugin-vibe-crew .claude/plugins/vibe-crew</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ── Workflow ──────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Workflow</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What's the difference between Tier 1 and Tier 2?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p><strong>Tier 1</strong> is the one-time project foundation: VISION.md, design system, TDR, architecture diagrams, roadmap, and CLAUDE.md. It runs sequentially and must complete before any source code can be written.</p>
          <p><strong>Tier 2</strong> is the iterative feature cycle: Plan &rarr; UI Design &rarr; Code &rarr; Test &rarr; Review &rarr; Docs. Each feature goes through this 6-phase process.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can I skip the foundation (Tier 1)?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>No. The phase gate hook blocks source code writes until the foundation is complete. This is intentional &mdash; writing code without architecture decisions leads to rework, inconsistency, and technical debt. The foundation typically takes 15-30 minutes and saves hours of rework later.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How do I add VibeCrew to an existing project?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Use <code>/onboard</code> instead of <code>/new-project</code>. The Code Auditor agent analyzes your codebase, extracts conventions, derives a design system from existing styles, and generates a CLAUDE.md.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What does /run-backlog do?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p><code>/run-backlog</code> auto-executes features from your backlog by priority. It runs the full Tier 2 cycle for each feature, including code review, and stops on failure for human review. VibeCrew forces a <code>/compact</code> between features to prevent context rot during long autonomous runs.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What are the UI and business pattern libraries?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>VibeCrew includes two pattern libraries that guide agents toward production-quality output. The <a href={url('/ui-patterns.html')}>UI Pattern Guide</a> covers component implementation &mdash; forms, navigation, data display, feedback, overlays, and keyboard interactions. The <a href={url('/business-patterns.html')}>Business Pattern Guide</a> covers conversion-oriented patterns &mdash; landing pages, pricing, onboarding, empty states, trust signals, and retention.</p>
          <p>Agents automatically reference these patterns when building features, so you get proper error states, accessible interactions, and conversion-optimized layouts without having to specify them.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ── Cost & Performance ────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Cost &amp; Performance</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How much does VibeCrew cost to run?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>VibeCrew itself is free and open source. You need a Claude subscription or API access to use Claude Code:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Claude Pro ($20/month) or Max ($100-200/month)</strong> &mdash; recommended for most users. Fixed monthly cost, no per-token billing. Max gives higher usage limits for intensive workflows like <code>/run-backlog</code>.</li>
            <li><strong>Claude API</strong> &mdash; pay-per-token alternative. A typical foundation session costs $5-15, feature sessions $2-8 depending on complexity. Use <code>/cost</code> for real-time spending and <code>/profile</code> to configure cost guardrails.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>Both are fully supported. Start with Pro if you&rsquo;re unsure.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How does VibeCrew keep token costs down?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Several mechanisms: (1) worktree agents isolate research tokens from the main session, (2) hooks enforce rules at zero token cost, (3) MCP servers like Context7 replace pasting docs (~1,500 tokens saved per query), (4) CLAUDE.md is kept under 200 lines, and (5) forced compaction between features in <code>/run-backlog</code> prevents context rot.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is the Vibe Score?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>A per-session quality metric starting at 100. Points are deducted for prompt churn, tool loops, skipped tests, and documentation drift. Bonuses are awarded for complete artifacts, high cache utilization, TDD discipline, and clean sessions. Range: 0-130. See <a href={url('/self-improving.html')}>Self-Improving System</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <!-- ── Community & Contributing ──────────────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Community &amp; Contributing</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How can I contribute or suggest features?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>VibeCrew is open source and community-driven. You can:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Propose features</strong> &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/fabkrum/vibe-crew/issues/new?labels=enhancement">open a GitHub issue</a> with your idea</li>
            <li><strong>Report bugs</strong> &mdash; <a href="https://github.com/fabkrum/vibe-crew/issues/new?labels=bug">file a bug report</a> with your <code>/status</code> output</li>
            <li><strong>Share your project</strong> &mdash; built something with VibeCrew? <a href="https://github.com/fabkrum/vibe-crew/discussions/categories/show-and-tell">show it off</a> in Discussions</li>
            <li><strong>Submit a PR</strong> &mdash; bug fixes, documentation improvements, and new templates are welcome</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Where can I see what others have built?</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Check the <a href="https://github.com/fabkrum/vibe-crew/discussions/categories/show-and-tell">Show &amp; Tell</a> section in GitHub Discussions. Users share their projects, screenshots, and lessons learned. It&rsquo;s also a great place to find inspiration for your next build.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <!-- ── Troubleshooting ──────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <h3>Troubleshooting</h3>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>The phase gate is blocking my writes</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>This means the Tier 1 foundation isn't complete yet. Run <code>/status</code> to see which foundation steps are missing, then run <code>/new-project</code> (or <code>/onboard</code> for existing projects) to complete them. The phase gate only blocks source code writes &mdash; config files, documentation, and <code>.vibecrew/</code> state files are always writable.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>My session state is corrupted</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Run <code>/recover-state</code> to clear lock files and repair corrupted JSON. If that doesn't work, you can manually delete the <code>.vibecrew/locks/</code> directory. Your backlog, session logs, and scores are never deleted during recovery.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Notifications aren't working</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ol>
            <li>Test <code>terminal-notifier</code> directly: <code>terminal-notifier -message "test"</code></li>
            <li>Check macOS: System Settings &rarr; Notifications &rarr; terminal-notifier</li>
            <li>Verify <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code> has <code>"notifications": true</code></li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>An MCP server isn't connecting</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <p>Default servers (Context7, Chrome DevTools, Playwright) work via <code>npx</code> &mdash; try restarting Claude Code. For conditional servers (Supabase, Stripe, etc.), ensure the required environment variables are set. Run <code>bash scripts/enable-mcp-server.sh &lt;name&gt; enable</code> to activate. All MCP servers are optional &mdash; agents gracefully fall back to built-in tools.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Vibe Score seems inaccurate</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ul>
            <li><strong>New projects:</strong> Scores are less meaningful in the first few sessions as the system establishes baselines</li>
            <li><strong>Complex features:</strong> Large features naturally have more tool calls and corrections &mdash; the score accounts for this but may seem low</li>
            <li><strong>Override deductions:</strong> If the coach flags something unavoidable, say so during the coaching dialogue &mdash; it factors into the next proposal</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>/heal can't fetch CI logs</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ol>
            <li>Verify the CLI is authenticated: <code>gh auth status</code> (GitHub) or <code>glab auth status</code> (GitLab)</li>
            <li>Ensure the repo has a remote: <code>git remote -v</code></li>
            <li>Check that a recent CI run exists: <code>gh run list --limit 5</code> (GitHub) or <code>glab ci list</code> (GitLab)</li>
            <li>If the failure is on a different branch, switch to it first</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>/simplify shows no suggestions</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ul>
            <li>The agent only analyzes files changed on the current feature branch vs. the default branch</li>
            <li>Verify you're on a feature branch (not main/master): <code>git branch --show-current</code></li>
            <li>Check that source files were changed (config and docs are excluded)</li>
            <li>The minimum files threshold is configurable in <code>.vibecrew/config.json</code> under <code>simplify.min_files_for_analysis</code></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>/replay can't find eligible sessions</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ul>
            <li>Only sessions with a Vibe Score &ge; 70 are eligible for template extraction</li>
            <li>Verify session logs exist in <code>.vibecrew/sessions/</code> and score files in <code>.vibecrew/scores/</code></li>
            <li>Run <code>/wrap</code> at the end of sessions to generate the required data</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Gamification not showing after /wrap</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ol>
            <li>Check that gamification is enabled: <code>jq '.gamification.enabled' .vibecrew/config.json</code> should return <code>true</code></li>
            <li>Verify <code>.vibecrew/gamification.json</code> exists. If not, run <code>/setup</code> to create it</li>
            <li>Check that the gamification scripts are executable: <code>ls -la scripts/award-xp.sh</code></li>
            <li>Run a script manually to debug: <code>bash scripts/award-xp.sh</code> &mdash; it outputs JSON with the XP breakdown</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>/quiz shows "Unlocks at Level 3"</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ul>
            <li>The <code>/quiz</code> command requires Level 3 (approximately 3&ndash;4 completed sessions)</li>
            <li>Check your level: <code>jq '.level' .vibecrew/gamification.json</code></li>
            <li>Keep running <code>/wrap</code> at the end of sessions to earn XP and level up</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Streak broke unexpectedly</summary>
        <div class="faq-answer">
          <ul>
            <li>Streaks increment on calendar days where <code>/wrap</code> runs. If you didn&rsquo;t wrap, the day doesn&rsquo;t count</li>
            <li>Weekends never break streaks. Check if you missed a <strong>weekday</strong></li>
            <li>Grace days reset on the 1st of each month: <code>jq '.streak.grace_days_remaining' .vibecrew/gamification.json</code></li>
            <li>If the streak counter seems wrong, check <code>.streak.last_session_date</code> in gamification.json</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>

</DocsLayout>

<style>
  .faq-item {
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .faq-item summary {
    padding: 1rem 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text);
    cursor: pointer;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: space-between;
    transition: background 0.15s;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .faq-item summary::-webkit-details-marker {
    display: none;
  }

  .faq-item summary::after {
    content: '+';
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
    transition: transform 0.2s;
  }

  .faq-item[open] summary::after {
    content: '\2212';
  }

  .faq-item summary:hover {
    background: var(--color-surface);
  }

  .faq-answer {
    padding: 0 1.25rem 1rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-subtle);
  }

  .faq-answer p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
